package acp

// AgentRunner — the ACP port the engine calls through to drive an agent, plus
// the deterministic MockRunner that marks the GALA-TEAM extraction seam.
//
// Stays pure-data and transport-neutral: no gala_tui, no app/ui, no IO. See the
// package doc in messages.gala for the full invariant list.

import (
    . "martianoff/gala/collection_immutable"
)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Run transcript — the chosen stream representation
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// STREAM-REPRESENTATION CHOICE: an event-list result (a pure RunTranscript),
// NOT a pull-based sequence or a callback sink.
//
// The contract says `Submit` yields a stream of ProgressEvents that terminates
// in exactly one of: a Result, a Clarification, or an abort. We model that
// "stream" as plain immutable data: an ordered Array of intermediate steps plus
// a single sealed terminus (`RunOutcome`) as its own non-optional field.
//
// Why this shape:
//   - Purity / transport-neutrality. A pull-iterator or callback sink implies
//     suspension and side effects — exactly the IO the ACP must NOT contain.
//     A value carries no effects, so the contract stays clean and the engine
//     can keep its IO at the edges (Cmd[T]/Async) where it belongs.
//   - The terminus invariant becomes STRUCTURAL. `Final` is a separate,
//     always-present sealed field, so "every run ends in exactly one outcome" is
//     guaranteed by the type — no scanning an array hoping the last element ends
//     it, and every consumer must handle all three terminus cases.
//   - Determinism + testability. Equal inputs give an equal transcript value,
//     which is what makes MockRunner round-trips and golden fixtures trivial.
//
// A genuinely incremental, back-pressured stream (for the live UI ticker) is an
// EDGE concern: the gala_tui layer adapts this transcript — or a future
// chunked/Cmd-based variant — into messages. That adaptation does not belong in
// the transport-neutral ACP, so it is intentionally absent here.

// THE CLARIFICATION-RESOLUTION LOOP (why the port stays one-shot/stateless):
//
// The runner never holds a live reply channel. A run that needs an answer simply
// ENDS, and the question is threaded back in on a fresh assignment — the exact
// same stateless pattern as retry-via-PriorFailure:
//
//   1. Agent cannot proceed without an answer → it ends the run with
//      `OutcomeClarification(question)`. No further steps; the run is over.
//   2. Engine surfaces the question to the user (UI screen S5).
//   3. Engine builds a FRESH TaskAssignment for the same Unit with the user's
//      reply appended to `Answers` (see messages.gala) and re-Submits it.
//   4. Agent now has the answer in `assignment.Answers` and proceeds — typically
//      to `OutcomeResult`, or to another `OutcomeClarification` if it needs more.
//
// This keeps Submit a pure one-shot TaskAssignment -> RunTranscript: no
// suspension, no bidirectional session, no IO inside the contract.

// RunStep is one non-terminal item the agent emits while working. Sealed with a
// single case for now: a run terminus is deliberately NOT a RunStep, so a step
// can never be mistaken for the end of a run. One case still leaves room for
// future non-terminal step kinds without implying a run can end mid-array.
sealed type RunStep {
    case StepProgress(Event ProgressEvent)
}

// RunOutcome is the sealed terminus of a run — exactly how a Submit ends. Sealed
// so every consumer must handle all three ways a run can finish.
sealed type RunOutcome {
    case OutcomeResult(Value Result)              // agent produced a translation
    case OutcomeClarification(Value Clarification) // agent needs an answer; run ends here
    case OutcomeAborted(Reason string)             // wholesale failure / nothing produced / cancelled mid-run
}

// RunTranscript is the full outcome of a Submit: the ordered intermediate steps
// followed by exactly one sealed terminus.
struct RunTranscript(
    Steps Array[RunStep],
    Final RunOutcome,
)

// CancelOutcome is the result of asking the runner to cancel a task. A pure
// acknowledgement value — the actual teardown is the implementation's IO, done
// before it returns this.
sealed type CancelOutcome {
    case Cancelled()  // the task was running and has been stopped
    case NotRunning() // no live task with that id (already done / never seen)
}

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The AgentRunner port
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// AgentRunner is the ACP port: the engine's only handle on an agent backend.
//
// It is modeled as a struct of function values rather than a Go-style interface
// — the GALA-idiomatic way to express a port (cf. gala_tui's `Program`, which
// carries `Update`/`View` the same way). A backend is a value you can build,
// pass, and swap; tests inject a MockRunner with no subtyping ceremony.
//
//   Submit: TaskAssignment -> RunTranscript (the stream-as-data, see above).
//   Cancel: TaskId         -> CancelOutcome.
//
// The function values themselves are pure from the ACP's point of view; a real
// backend performs its IO inside them and hands back the resulting value. Both
// signatures stay free of gala_tui / app/ui types.
struct AgentRunner(
    Submit func(TaskAssignment) RunTranscript,
    Cancel func(TaskId) CancelOutcome,
)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EXTENSION SEAM — where GALA-TEAM's extracted implementation plugs in
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// MockRunner is the seam marker and the default AgentRunner this contract ships.
// It satisfies the port DETERMINISTICALLY so the engine and its tests have a
// real value to run against before the production backend exists. Its Submit
// always terminates in OutcomeResult — the success path.
//
// GALA-TEAM's extracted runner replaces THIS constructor (or sits beside it as
// e.g. `func GalaTeamRunner(cfg ...) AgentRunner`). The expected production
// behavior, which the mock only stubs:
//   - Submit: actually dispatch the unit to a live agent, collecting real
//     ProgressEvents into Steps and ending in the appropriate RunOutcome —
//     OutcomeResult on success (raising UnsupportedFlags for any non-faithful
//     construct instead of guessing), OutcomeClarification when it needs an
//     answer, or OutcomeAborted on wholesale failure.
//   - Cancel: signal the live task to stop and report whether one was running.
//
// Nothing here invents GALA-TEAM internals — the seam is exactly a mock plus
// this description of the contract the real implementation must honor.
func MockRunner() AgentRunner =
    AgentRunner(
        Submit = (assignment) => RunTranscript(
            Steps = mockSteps(assignment.Task),
            Final = OutcomeResult(Value = Result(
                Task       = assignment.Task,
                TargetText = "// mock translation",
                Rationale  = "deterministic mock output (extraction seam stub)",
                Flags      = EmptyArray[UnsupportedFlag](),
            )),
        ),
        Cancel = (_) => Cancelled(),
    )

// ScriptedRunner is a test-facing variant of the seam: it lets a caller pin the
// exact terminus a Submit produces, so each RunOutcome case can be exercised
// THROUGH THE PORT (not just via hand-built transcripts). The progress steps are
// the same faithful Reading->Emitting shape; only the terminus is scripted.
// Cancel reports the supplied CancelOutcome so NotRunning is reachable too.
func ScriptedRunner(outcome RunOutcome, cancel CancelOutcome) AgentRunner =
    AgentRunner(
        Submit = (assignment) => RunTranscript(
            Steps = mockSteps(assignment.Task),
            Final = outcome,
        ),
        Cancel = (_) => cancel,
    )

// mockSteps is the shared, faithful-shaped progress prefix: a Reading then an
// Emitting ProgressEvent, echoing the task id so the join key is observable.
func mockSteps(task TaskId) Array[RunStep] =
    ArrayOf[RunStep](
        StepProgress(Event = ProgressEvent(
            Task  = task,
            Phase = Reading(),
            Note  = "reading source unit",
        )),
        StepProgress(Event = ProgressEvent(
            Task  = task,
            Phase = Emitting(),
            Note  = "emitting target text",
        )),
    )
